WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.720 --> 00:00:04.360
This is not a test.

00:00:05.200 --> 00:00:08.980
In recent years, there's been much talk of
American schools having been consumed

00:00:08.980 --> 00:00:12.920
by "testing mania." Heck, I've shared
some of these concerns myself.

00:00:12.920 --> 00:00:16.160
We especially hear these
concerns raised in comparison to

00:00:16.170 --> 00:00:20.130
supposed role models, like Finland. But
how much of an international outlier,

00:00:20.130 --> 00:00:26.060
really, is the US when it comes to
emphasis on testing? Turns out, not so much.

00:00:26.060 --> 00:00:32.180
A recent OECD analysis shows that
in 2015, 83 percent of US teachers put major

00:00:32.180 --> 00:00:36.920
emphasis on classroom tests to monitor
student progress. That sounds like a lot,

00:00:36.920 --> 00:00:42.210
but it wasn't much higher than the OECD
average of 77 percent. Meanwhile,

00:00:42.210 --> 00:00:48.560
in science, in both 2007 and 2015, US
teachers put less emphasis on tests

00:00:48.560 --> 00:00:53.860
than did their OECD counterparts. In other
words, the US was a bit above the international

00:00:53.860 --> 00:00:58.900
norm in math and a bit lower in science.
If we think the international community

00:00:58.900 --> 00:01:02.120
is a reasonable benchmark,
the data suggests that concerns

00:01:02.120 --> 00:01:06.980
about over emphasis on testing
have been somewhat overblown.

00:01:06.980 --> 00:01:11.440
Do you think American schools are obsessed
with testing? Let us know in our poll.

00:01:11.440 --> 00:01:15.860
Also, let us know what other topics you'd like
our scholars to cover in 60 seconds

00:01:15.860 --> 00:01:22.580
and be sure to LIKE and subscribe for more
research and videos from AEI.

